ROOM NOISE WARNING: this is the biggest practical risk in the whole talk, more than the maths. Test this exact slide in the actual room beforehand, not just at your desk. Have a pre-recorded spectrogram screenshot of the same note ready as a fallback slide if the live mic doesn’t read clearly over classroom noise.
Let the gap (or satisfying lack of one) between the predicted number from the last slide and the real measured peak here land in silence for a second before you say anything.

Imagine an exhibition trail around the Botanic Gardens, with a fixed set of stops – numbered markers a visitor could walk round. If you wanted to visit every stop and come back to where you started, covering as little ground as possible, which order should you go in?
This is the Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP) – one of the most famous problems in the whole of mathematics and computer science. It sounds almost too simple to be interesting: just try every possible order of stops and keep the shortest one. The catch is how many orders there are. With 5 stops there are only 12 distinct routes to check. With 10 stops there are already 181,440. With 20 stops there are over 60 quadrillion – so many that a computer checking a billion routes every single second would still need about two years to get through them all. Trying every option, which works fine for a handful of stops, becomes hopeless almost immediately – and no computer, however fast, escapes that by brute force alone.
That collision – a problem you can state to a ten-year-old, that defeats every computer on Earth once it gets big enough – is exactly why mathematicians and computer scientists still work on it. Real versions of this problem sit behind delivery route planning, circuit board drilling, and DNA sequencing.
The map below shows five real stops from our Botanic Gardens exhibition trail: 1. The Glass House Cactus Garden, 2. The Chalet, 3. The Good Grief Garden, 4. The Patterson Education Centre, and 5. The Eddie Kemp Pavilion.

Click stops to select them – choose a handful (at least 3) to build a route around.
Switch to “Adds the next stop to your route” and click your selected stops in whatever order you think gives the shortest trip. That’s your manually-built route.
Compare it against a fast algorithm route (nearest neighbour, optionally cleaned up with a technique called 2-opt), and, since there are only 5 stops here, the exact optimal route found by checking every possibility.
Use the toggle to switch which route is drawn on the map, and watch how close your own guess gets to optimal.

If you’re visiting the exhibition in person, look out for a QR code at each stop. Scanning it opens this page and automatically checks that stop in on your phone – your visited stops build up under “Your physical visit” in the sidebar, in the order you actually walked them, and once you’ve found all five the app shows you how your real walking route compares to the mathematically optimal one.



